{
  "dom_id": "step-q47651",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. $det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that if we set \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  \n\nthen the fourth part of the theorem is true. Hence,  all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III give us a true statement: \n\n- *If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
